{"name":"Laird","hex":"#79853c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Laird","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname.","A place in Canada:\nA township and village in Algoma District, northern Ontario.\nA rural municipality, the Rural Municipality of Laird No. 404, in central Saskatchewan.\nA village within the rural municipality in Saskatchewan, named after David Laird."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Laird"}],"entries":[{"title":"Laird","blocks":[{"text":"Laird is a Scottish word for minor lord and is a designation that applies to an owner of a large, long-established Scottish estate. In the traditional Scottish order of precedence, a laird ranked below a baron and above a gentleman. This rank was held only by those holding official recognition in a territorial designation by the Lord Lyon King of Arms. They are usually styled [name] [surname] of [lairdship]. However, since \"laird\" is a courtesy title, it has no formal status in law."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laird","wikidata":"Q1515878"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Mid-16thC_Renaissance_carving_from_Killochan_Castle%2C_Ayrshire.JPG/500px-Mid-16thC_Renaissance_carving_from_Killochan_Castle%2C_Ayrshire.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Mid-16thC_Renaissance_carving_from_Killochan_Castle%2C_Ayrshire.JPG/1280px-Mid-16thC_Renaissance_carving_from_Killochan_Castle%2C_Ayrshire.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Mid-16thC_Renaissance_carving_from_Killochan_Castle%2C_Ayrshire.JPG/500px-Mid-16thC_Renaissance_carving_from_Killochan_Castle%2C_Ayrshire.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Carving believed to depict a 16th-century Scottish laird","link":